  "narrative": "The new WRN evidence strongly supports the already accepted MSI-high association and makes independent validation the highest-value next test. For H57, the signed batch_a main effect is R41 = +0.824 [0.766, 0.882]; because the hypothesis direction is -1, this corresponds to a strongly negative raw exposed-minus-comparator difference, i.e. MSI-high tumors in batch_a have much stronger WRN dependency. H63 gives a similarly large batch_b main effect, R42 = +0.789 [0.726, 0.852]. These are not small technical artifacts: they are on the order of three standard deviations of the WRN score distribution and far exceed the 10% clinical-significance benchmark under any sensible scale. H64 shows that restricting to high mutation burden does not collapse the effect, R43 = +0.775 [0.737, 0.812], which argues against a simple explanation in which MSI-high is only a proxy for global hypermutation. H65 shows that restricting to higher Cas9 activity also leaves the effect very large, R44 = +0.776 [0.728, 0.824], reducing concern that this is an artifact of weak CRISPR performance, although Cas9 activity is missing for part of the dataset and selection into measured high-activity screens cannot be fully ruled out. H66 shows that the effect is not confined to KRAS-mutant co-mutational context, R45 = +0.792 [0.759, 0.825] in KRAS-wild-type tumors. Taken with the overall estimate R6 = +0.790 and the Avana stratum R18 = +0.813, the WRN result is highly internally robust except that the non-Avana WRN stratum remains unestimable due to only 10 exposed observations in R27.  \n\nThe role-free marker-D controls are reassuring for specificity. H59 gives R46 = +0.012 [-0.011, 0.035], H60 gives R47 = +0.002 [-0.030, 0.035], H61 gives R48 = -0.001 [-0.032, 0.031], and H62 gives R49 = +0.005 [-0.027, 0.038]. These are near null and several orders smaller than the biomarker associations. I cannot exclude a very small marker-D association, but the evidence does not support marker-D as a driver of the huge WRN, SMARCA2, NFE2L2, or USP7 effects, and these controls argue against a generic role-free-marker or broad cohort-structure explanation. I therefore reject the marker-D association hypotheses and close these controls.  \n\nThe SMARCA2 batch caveat is now resolved as a technical caveat rather than a substantive challenge. H58 gives the batch_a main effect of SMARCA4 loss on SMARCA2 as R50 = +0.747 [0.709, 0.785], again enormous. This means that although the small batch_a interaction in R32 was opposite the main-effect direction, the effect within batch_a itself remains very large. I therefore close H50. H67 similarly shows that SMARCA4 loss remains strongly associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency among TP53-wild-type tumors, R51 = +0.787 [0.766, 0.809], reducing concern that the SMARCA2 result is merely entangled with TP53 co-loss.  \n\nThe EGFR driver-exclusive refinement remains unresolved. H71 gives R52 = +0.021 [-0.017, 0.060]. The direction is consistent with stronger EGFR dependency, but the point estimate is small, the interval includes both null and modestly larger effects, and the exposed cell is modest. This is not enough to accept an EGFR dependency association. Because many EGFR stratum tests are small and uncertain, I defer H71 and do not request validation for EGFR.  \n\nGiven that WRN now has a very large overall effect, library-stratum support, both largest batch main effects, high-mutation-burden support, high-Cas9 support, KRAS-wild-type support, and null role-free marker controls, the main remaining concern is discovery multiplicity and residual confounding. An independent validation split is the best next evidence. I request independent validation of H6. This request is for replication of a dataset association only, not a claim of causality or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Post-appraise updated state: Four large accepted associations exist. TP53 loss -> much weaker USP7 dependency: H11 accepted/closed, discovery R11 +0.802 [0.782,0.822], validation R13 +0.800 [0.769,0.832]. KEAP1 loss -> much weaker NFE2L2 dependency: H17 accepted/closed, R8/R26. SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency: H7 accepted/closed, R7/R39; Avana H28 accepted/closed R19/R40; non-Avana H44 accepted/closed R28; ARID1A-wild-type H36 accepted/closed R35; TP53-wild-type H67 accepted/closed R51; batch_a main effect H58 accepted/closed R50; batch_a interaction caveat H50 rejected/closed R32. Marker-E/F/D controls for SMARCA2 null: R30, R34, R47.  \n\nMSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency is now much more mature but not independently validated. Main H6 remains accepted active pending validation: R6 +0.790 [0.760,0.819]. Supporting robustness accepted/closed: Avana H27 R18 +0.813 [0.778,0.847]; batch_a H57 R41 +0.824 [0.766,0.882]; batch_b H63 R42 +0.789 [0.726,0.852]; high mutation burden H64 R43 +0.775 [0.737,0.812]; high Cas9 H65 R44 +0.776 [0.728,0.824]; KRAS-wild-type H66 R45 +0.792 [0.759,0.825]. Marker-E H41 null R21; marker-F H51 null R33; marker-D H59 null R46. Non-Avana WRN stratum H43 remains invalid/deferred because exposed n=10 in R27. WRN batch_a interaction H49 was inconclusive and is closed as low priority because batch main effects are large. Validation request made for H6 to address multiplicity and residual confounding.  \n\nOther marker controls: marker-D SMARCA2 H60 null/closed R47; marker-D NFE2L2 H61 null/closed R48; marker-D USP7 H62 null/closed R49. These reduce concern that very large dependency effects are nonspecific cohort/assay structure.  \n\nEGFR remains unresolved and should not be accepted without a robust clinically meaningful stratum effect and preferably validation. Overall R3 +0.025 [-0.000,0.051]; KRAS-wild-type R22 +0.016; TP53-wild-type pending/older unresolved; TP53-loss R37 +0.025; KRAS/TP53-wild-type pending; driver-exclusive adenocarcinoma H71 R52 +0.021 [-0.017,0.060], deferred.  \n\nNegative or low-support broad associations so far: KRAS mutation not associated with stronger KRAS dependency (H1); STK11 loss not associated with KRAS dependency (H54); BRAF V600E not associated with BRAF dependency (H2); ERBB2 amplification not associated with ERBB2 dependency (H4); TP53 loss not associated with stronger MDM2 dependency (H5); PIK3CA mutation not associated with stronger STAT3 dependency (H12); MYC amplification not associated with stronger DHX9/RPL11/POLR2A dependency (H9/H10/H19); BRCA2 loss not associated with stronger KIF18A dependency (H20); ARID1A loss overall not associated with SMARCA2 dependency (H18), and ARID1A loss in SMARCA4-wild-type tumors null (H37).  \n\nNext priorities: (1) assess independent validation of H6 when it arrives; if concordant, close H6; if discordant, reopen WRN and inspect batch/library/co-mutation strata. (2) Run remaining specificity/context controls already registered: H68 SMARCA4 loss vs POLR2A dependency; H69 TP53-loss/USP7 in KRAS-wild-type; H70 KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 in KRAS-wild-type; H42 marker-E USP7; H47 marker-E NFE2L2; H56 SMARCA4 loss with ARID1A co-loss; H55 STAT3/PTEN; H72/H73 NF1-loss pathway controls; H74/H75 marker-F USP7/NFE2L2. (3) New proposal registers MSI-high vs POLR2A dependency as an outcome-specificity control: if near null, it would support WRN-specificity rather than a global hypermutation/CRISPR artifact. All accepted claims remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or externally generalizable.",
